# Oceania batch (1606-2023): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"oc-duyfken": dict(
 bluf="The Dutch East India Company sent navigator Willem Janszoon in the Duyfken to find trade south of New Guinea. He charted about 300 km of Cape York's west coast in 1606, lost several crew in clashes with Aboriginal people and reported nothing worth a return voyage. The company left the continent alone, and the first recorded European contact with Australia led to no settlement for another 180 years.",
 now="Dutch indifference left Australia to its Aboriginal peoples until Britain, with a different purpose, planted a penal colony in 1788. The 1606 contact is where the continent's two records, at least 50,000 years of Aboriginal occupation and four centuries of European writing, first touch, and how Australia tells that story is part of its politics.",
 story="The Dutch East India Company (VOC) ran its Asian trade from Batavia and sent ships to probe for new markets. In early 1606 Dutch navigator Willem Janszoon took the Duyfken south from New Guinea and charted about 300 km of the west coast of Cape York Peninsula. Clashes with Aboriginal people left several of his crew dead. He reported swamps, hostile inhabitants and nothing to trade.",
 why="The VOC was searching for new trade and the rumoured Great South Land. A coast with nothing to sell gave it no reason to return.",
 then="Dutch ships charted much of Australia's west and north coasts over the next 40 years and named the land New Holland. The VOC found no profit in it and made no settlement. Britain's claim to the east coast in 1770 met no Dutch objection.",
 fact="Janszoon took Cape York for part of New Guinea; Spanish navigator Luis Váez de Torres sailed through the strait that separates them the same year, and neither knew of the other."),
"oc-tasman": dict(
 bluf="The Dutch East India Company sent navigator Abel Tasman to find the Great South Land and a route to South America. He found Tasmania and New Zealand in 1642, lost four sailors to a Māori attack at Golden Bay and landed on neither. The company judged both worthless, and New Zealand stayed Māori for another 127 years until British navigator James Cook arrived in 1769.",
 now="Empires followed profit rather than discovery: the Dutch saw nothing to trade and stayed away, which is why Britain rather than the Netherlands colonised New Zealand in 1840.",
 story="Dutch navigator Abel Tasman sailed from Batavia in August 1642 with two ships. He sighted Tasmania in November 1642 and named it Van Diemen's Land, then New Zealand's South Island in December. At Golden Bay on 19 December Ngāti Tūmatakōkiri warriors in canoes attacked a ship's boat and killed four sailors. Tasman named the place Murderers' Bay and sailed up the North Island's west coast without landing.",
 why="The VOC wanted the Great South Land and a route to South America. Tasman's instructions put trade before exploration, and nothing he saw promised trade.",
 then="The VOC filed the voyage and sent no follow-up. No European returned to New Zealand until British navigator James Cook in 1769. Dutch cartographers gave the islands the name Nova Zeelandia.",
 fact="Tasman called the land Staten Landt, believing it joined South America; no one from his ships set foot on New Zealand."),
"oc-cook": dict(
 bluf="The Admiralty sent Lieutenant James Cook to observe the transit of Venus from Tahiti and then, under sealed orders, to search for the southern continent. He charted New Zealand's coasts in 1769-70 and Australia's east coast in 1770 and claimed the latter for Britain. The charts gave Britain the claim it used to found a penal colony in 1788, which began the dispossession of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.",
 now="Cook's claim treated the east coast as land open to the Crown; the convict colony of 1788 and the dispossession of the Aboriginal nations followed from it, and the High Court overturned the legal fiction behind it only in 1992.",
 story="British navigator Lieutenant James Cook sailed the Endeavour from Plymouth in 1768 with naturalist Joseph Banks aboard. He charted New Zealand's coasts in 1769-70, then reached Australia's east coast and anchored in Botany Bay in late April 1770, where Gweagal men confronted his landing party and were fired on. In August, at Possession Island off Cape York, he claimed the whole east coast for Britain.",
 why="The Royal Society wanted the transit of Venus observed from Tahiti to measure the solar system. Secret Admiralty orders told Cook to look for the southern continent and take possession of convenient sites.",
 then="Naturalist Joseph Banks told a parliamentary committee in 1779 that Botany Bay would suit a penal colony. The First Fleet arrived in 1788.",
 fact="Cook's secret instructions told him to take possession 'with the consent of the natives'; he claimed the east coast without asking."),
"oc-firstfleet": dict(
 bluf="Britain needed somewhere to send convicts after American independence closed the colonies to them, and wanted a foothold in the Pacific. New South Wales Governor Arthur Phillip landed about 750 convicts at Sydney Cove on 26 January 1788, and the colony survived its first years of near-starvation. It founded settler Australia on Aboriginal land that British law treated as belonging to no one, and that premise governed Australian land law until 1992.",
 now="The colony fixed the terms of Australian settlement: Crown land granted to settlers, Aboriginal peoples without legal title, a convict labour force. The doctrine that the land belonged to no one stood until the High Court overturned it in 1992.",
 story="Eleven ships left Portsmouth in May 1787 with about 750 convicts and their marine guard. New South Wales Governor Arthur Phillip found Botany Bay unsuitable and raised the British flag at Sydney Cove on 26 January 1788. The colony came close to starvation in its first years while it waited for supply ships. Smallpox broke out among the local Eora people in April 1789 and killed a large share of them.",
 why="After 1783 Britain could no longer ship convicts to America and its prison hulks were full. A settlement at Sydney also gave Britain a base in the Pacific and a source of flax and timber.",
 then="Settlement spread along the rivers and, after 1813, across the Blue Mountains. Frontier violence and disease reduced Aboriginal populations across the continent; about 162,000 convicts were transported by 1868. One reading holds the 1789 smallpox came from the colony's medical stocks or its ships; the other holds it arrived from the north with Macassan fishermen; the records settle neither.",
 fact="26 January, the day Phillip raised the flag, is Australia Day and to many Aboriginal Australians Invasion Day. Phillip was speared through the shoulder at Manly in 1790 and ordered no reprisal."),
"oc-kamehameha": dict(
 bluf="Hawaiian chief Kamehameha set out to bring all the islands under one ruler, with Western muskets, cannon and two British sailors as advisers. He conquered Maui and Oʻahu by 1795 and took Kauaʻi by submission in 1810. The kingdom he founded won recognition from Britain, France and the United States, held the islands together for 83 years and remains the legal basis of Native Hawaiian sovereignty claims.",
 now="The kingdom's existence as a recognised state is what Native Hawaiian sovereignty movements rest on: an overthrown government rather than a conquered tribe, which is the claim the US Congress acknowledged in 1993.",
 story="The Hawaiian Islands were divided among rival chiefdoms when British and American ships began calling after 1778. Kamehameha, a chief from the island of Hawaiʻi, acquired muskets and cannon through trade and kept the British sailors John Young and Isaac Davis as advisers. He took Maui and Oʻahu by 1795, the latter at the battle of Nuʻuanu. In 1810 Kauaʻi's ruler Kaumualiʻi accepted his authority, which completed the unification.",
 why="Rivalry among the chiefdoms came first. Western weapons gave the chief who got them first a decisive edge.",
 then="The kingdom traded sandalwood, then provisioned whalers, and adopted a constitution in 1840. Britain and France recognised its independence in 1843. American planters overthrew it in 1893.",
 fact="Kamehameha's Law of the Splintered Paddle, protecting travellers and non-combatants, is written into Hawaii's state constitution."),
"oc-musket": dict(
 bluf="Northern Māori iwi that traded first with European ships armed themselves with muskets to settle old scores with their rivals. Raids led by Ngāpuhi chief Hongi Hika from 1818 set off two decades of war in which perhaps 20,000 Māori died and whole districts were emptied or enslaved. The devastation made some chiefs receptive to British protection, which they accepted in the Treaty of Waitangi in 1840.",
 now="The wars redrew tribal boundaries that still govern land claims in New Zealand, and the disorder they left is why many chiefs saw advantage in a British governor in 1840.",
 story="Ships calling at the Bay of Islands after 1800 traded muskets for flax, timber, food and preserved heads. Ngāpuhi chief Hongi Hika led armed raids south from 1818 against iwi that had none. Other iwi rushed to arm, and the fighting spread across the North Island through the 1820s and 1830s. Perhaps 20,000 Māori died in two decades, and many were displaced or enslaved; the wars faded once every iwi held muskets.",
 why="Old grievances between iwi came first; the musket turned them into campaigns of annihilation. Access to European trade decided who struck first.",
 then="Ngāti Toa under chief Te Rauparaha moved south to Kapiti Island and Cook Strait. Missionaries and traders pressed Britain to intervene, and the disorder was one of the Crown's stated reasons for the treaty of 1840.",
 fact="Hongi Hika visited England in 1820, met King George IV, and traded many of his gifts for muskets in Sydney on the way home."),
"oc-myall": dict(
 bluf="New South Wales Governor George Gipps set out to show that the law protected Aboriginal people after stockmen murdered about 28 Wirrayaraay people at Myall Creek in June 1838. Seven of the killers were convicted and hanged, the only such hangings on the Australian frontier. Colonists' outrage at the verdict taught later killers to leave no witnesses, and the massacres that followed went unpunished and largely unrecorded.",
 now="Myall Creek fixed the rule that Aboriginal people were protected by law in principle and taught the frontier to kill out of sight in practice; the count of later massacres is still argued, and the argument is part of Australian politics.",
 story="Squatters were pushing sheep and cattle onto Aboriginal land in the Gwydir valley. On 10 June 1838 a party of about a dozen stockmen led by John Fleming tied up and killed about 28 Wirrayaraay people, mostly women, children and old men, at Myall Creek station and burned the bodies. New South Wales Governor George Gipps insisted on prosecution; a first jury acquitted the men and a second convicted seven, who were hanged in December 1838. Fleming escaped and was never tried.",
 why="Frontier violence spread with the pastoral expansion of the 1830s. Gipps and Attorney-General John Plunkett wanted a case that would show the law applied to everyone.",
 then="Colonists' outrage at the hangings made later prosecutions rare, and later massacres were covered up. One count, a map of frontier massacres compiled between 2017 and 2022, records more than 400 sites and more than 10,000 Aboriginal dead between 1788 and 1930; critics of the count hold that many entries rest on single or second-hand accounts and that the evidence supports a far lower figure.",
 fact="Descendants of the victims and of the perpetrators built a memorial at the site together in 2000."),
"oc-waitangi": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted authority over a lawless settler frontier and to forestall France; Māori chiefs wanted protection and trade. On 6 February 1840 Lieutenant-Governor William Hobson and the chiefs signed a treaty whose English text ceded sovereignty and whose Māori text granted governorship while preserving chiefly authority. The Crown governed on the English text, settlers took the land, and since 1975 the Māori text has been the basis for redress, which keeps the treaty at the centre of New Zealand politics.",
 now="Since 1975 the Waitangi Tribunal has investigated Crown breaches and settlements have returned land and money to many iwi; what the treaty means for sovereignty is contested in Parliament, where a bill to redefine its principles was introduced in 2024 and voted down in 2025.",
 story="New Zealand in 1839 had about 2,000 European settlers, whalers and traders and no government. Lieutenant-Governor William Hobson arrived in January 1840 with instructions to obtain sovereignty by consent. At Waitangi on 6 February about 40 chiefs signed; copies went round the country and more than 500 chiefs had signed by September. In the English text the chiefs ceded 'sovereignty'; in the Māori text, which nearly all of them signed, they granted 'kāwanatanga' (governorship) and kept 'tino rangatiratanga', full chieftainship over their lands, villages and treasures.",
 why="Britain wanted to control a settler frontier before the New Zealand Company's ships arrived, and to forestall a French claim. The chiefs wanted protection against settlers and the disorder left by the Musket Wars, and trade.",
 then="One reading holds the chiefs ceded sovereignty, as the English text says and as the Crown has acted since 1840; the other holds that the Māori text they signed granted the right to govern settlers and kept chiefly authority intact, so that sovereignty stayed with the chiefs, a finding the Waitangi Tribunal made for the northern chiefs in 2014. Settler demand for land led to the New Zealand Wars of 1845-72 and large confiscations; by 1900 Māori held only a small fraction of their land.",
 fact="Missionary Henry Williams and his son translated the English draft into Māori overnight on 4-5 February 1840; the day of signing, 6 February, is New Zealand's national day."),
"oc-nzwars": dict(
 bluf="Māori chiefs fought to keep their land and the authority the treaty had promised them; the colonial government fought to open the North Island to settlers. Ngāpuhi chief Hōne Heke's war in the north in 1845 ended in stalemate; the Taranaki and Waikato wars of the 1860s ended in Māori defeat and the confiscation of millions of acres. Settlers rather than Māori would govern New Zealand, and the confiscations are what Crown settlements since 1995 have apologised for.",
 now="The confiscations impoverished the iwi of Taranaki and Waikato for a century and are the core of the treaty settlements the Crown has negotiated since the 1990s, with apologies and compensation written into law.",
 story="Ngāpuhi chief Hōne Heke, the first chief to sign at Waitangi in 1840, cut down the British flagstaff at Kororāreka four times between July 1844 and March 1845 in protest at lost trade and authority, and the Northern War followed. Fighting broke out in Taranaki in 1860 over a disputed land sale, and in 1863 colonial and imperial forces on the orders of New Zealand Governor George Grey invaded the Waikato, the territory of the Māori King. Māori fought on both sides. The wars ended by 1872, after the government confiscated millions of acres under the New Zealand Settlements Act of 1863.",
 why="Settlers wanted land faster than Māori would sell it. Disputed sales and the Māori King movement's refusal to sell raised the question of who held authority under the treaty.",
 then="Confiscated land opened the Waikato and Taranaki to settlers, and the iwi concerned were left poor. Waikato-Tainui received a Crown apology and NZ$170 million in 1995, the first large settlement; others followed.",
 fact="Queen Elizabeth II signed the Waikato-Tainui settlement act in person in 1995, the first Crown apology to an iwi."),
"oc-gold": dict(
 bluf="Prospectors who had learned the signs in California went looking for gold in Australia, and the colonial governments, fearing a labour exodus, offered rewards for finding it. Gold was found near Bathurst in February 1851 and in Victoria months later, and Australia's population roughly quadrupled in 20 years. A penal colony became an immigrant society, and hostility to Chinese diggers produced the first of the laws that became the White Australia policy.",
 now="Gold made Victoria rich and populous, filled the colonies with free migrants who demanded the vote, and produced anti-Chinese laws from 1855 that set the pattern for the Immigration Restriction Act of 1901.",
 story="Prospector Edward Hargraves, back from California, found gold near Bathurst in February 1851; the New South Wales government announced it in May. Finds at Ballarat and Bendigo in Victoria followed within months. Diggers came from Britain, Ireland, Europe, America and China. Australia's population grew from about 430,000 in 1851 to 1.7 million in 1871, and Melbourne became one of the richest cities in the world.",
 why="Prospectors from California recognised the signs. Governments offered rewards because free men were leaving for California and the colonies needed them.",
 then="Victoria taxed Chinese arrivals from 1855 and other colonies followed; miners attacked Chinese camps at Lambing Flat in 1861. The diggers' numbers forced the colonies to widen the franchise within a decade.",
 fact="The Welcome Stranger nugget, found at Moliagul in Victoria in 1869, weighed about 72 kg and remains the largest alluvial nugget ever found."),
"oc-eureka": dict(
 bluf="Ballarat's miners wanted the licence fee abolished, the police raids ended and the vote; the Victorian government wanted the licence enforced. Troops stormed the miners' stockade at dawn on 3 December 1854 and killed about 22 diggers. A Melbourne jury acquitted every miner charged with treason, the licence was replaced within a year and Victoria gave men the vote in 1857; the revolt failed in the field and won its demands.",
 now="Eureka's acquittals showed the colonial government that juries sided with the diggers, which made manhood suffrage in Victoria in 1857 the path of least resistance; the Southern Cross flag has been a protest symbol since.",
 story="Victoria's goldfields charged every digger 30 shillings a month for a licence, enforced by police 'digger hunts', and gave him no vote. In October 1854 a hotel keeper was acquitted of killing a miner, and diggers burned his hotel. Under Irish-born digger Peter Lalor the miners swore an oath under the Southern Cross flag and built a stockade at Eureka. Soldiers and police stormed it at dawn on 3 December 1854; about 22 diggers and a handful of soldiers died. A Melbourne jury acquitted all 13 miners tried for treason.",
 why="Heavy licence fees enforced by raids, with no vote to change them, gave the diggers a grievance. The acquittal of a hotel keeper for killing a miner turned grievance into revolt.",
 trig="The death of digger James Scobie outside Bentley's Eureka Hotel in October 1854 and the acquittal of its owner.",
 then="A royal commission replaced the licence with a £1 miner's right in 1855 that carried the vote. Victoria extended the vote to all adult men in 1857. Lalor entered the Victorian Parliament in 1855.",
 fact="Lalor lost an arm at the stockade and served as Speaker of the Victorian Legislative Assembly from 1880 to 1887."),
"oc-blackbird": dict(
 bluf="Queensland sugar planters wanted cheap labour for tropical plantations they believed white men would refuse. Recruiters brought tens of thousands of Pacific Islanders to Queensland between 1863 and 1904 on fixed contracts, many by deception or force, and thousands died. The new Commonwealth deported most of them after 1906 under the White Australia policy, so that labour exploitation and racial exclusion followed one another.",
 now="The trade fixed the pattern by which Australia imported non-white labour and then expelled it; the descendants of those who stayed were recognised as a distinct community, Australian South Sea Islanders, in 1994.",
 story="Queensland's planters began importing labour in 1863, when Sydney merchant Robert Towns brought the first shipload to his cotton plantation. From 1863 to 1904 recruiters took about 62,000 workers from Vanuatu, the Solomon Islands and other islands to Queensland on three-year contracts. Many were tricked or kidnapped, a practice called 'blackbirding', and thousands died of disease and overwork. The Commonwealth's Pacific Island Labourers Act of 1901 ordered their deportation.",
 why="Planters wanted cheap tropical labour. Recruiters were paid per head, and the islands lay beyond effective law.",
 then="Deportations ran from 1906, and the sugar industry was reserved for white labour under bounties. A minority won exemptions and stayed; their descendants were recognised in 1994.",
 fact="Townsville is named after Robert Towns, who brought the first Islander labourers to Queensland."),
"oc-lastconvicts": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted to keep transporting convicts; the eastern colonies, wanting free immigrants and self-government, had refused them since 1840, and only Western Australia still took them. The Hougoumont landed 280 convicts at Fremantle in January 1868, the last of about 162,000 sent since 1788. Transportation ended, and Australia's self-governing colonies went on as free settler societies with a convict past to live down.",
 now="The end of transportation closed the penal era and left the colonies to govern themselves as free societies; the shame attached to convict ancestry lasted a century and has since reversed into pride.",
 story="New South Wales stopped receiving convicts in 1840 and Van Diemen's Land in 1853, after campaigns by colonists. Western Australia, short of labour, asked for convicts in 1850. The Hougoumont reached Fremantle in January 1868 with 280 convicts, among them 62 Irish Fenian rebels. About 162,000 convicts had been transported to Australia since 1788.",
 why="The eastern colonies had refused convicts for a generation and British prisons had changed. Transportation was costly, and the colonists' objection was loud.",
 then="Six Fenians escaped from Fremantle in 1876 aboard the American whaler Catalpa. Western Australia's convict-built roads and public buildings remained.",
 fact="Perhaps one in five Australians descends from a convict."),
"oc-hawaii1893": dict(
 bluf="American sugar planters in Hawaii wanted annexation by the United States and duty-free access to its market; Hawaiian Queen Liliʻuokalani wanted a new constitution restoring royal power. A Committee of Safety of mostly American businessmen seized power on 17 January 1893 with US Marines landed by US Minister John Stevens. The monarchy was gone, annexation followed in 1898, and the US Congress acknowledged the overthrow as illegal in 1993 without reversing it.",
 now="The overthrow is the legal grievance on which Native Hawaiian sovereignty claims rest, acknowledged by the 1993 apology resolution and unresolved since.",
 story="The 1887 'Bayonet Constitution' had stripped the Hawaiian monarch of most power and limited the vote by wealth. Hawaiian Queen Liliʻuokalani moved in January 1893 to proclaim a new constitution restoring royal authority and Hawaiian voting rights. A Committee of Safety of mostly American businessmen declared a provisional government on 17 January 1893 under Sanford Dole; US Minister John Stevens had Marines landed from USS Boston the day before. The queen yielded under protest to avoid bloodshed.",
 why="Sugar planters wanted annexation to secure duty-free access to the American market after the 1890 US tariff ended their advantage. The queen's constitution threatened their control.",
 then="US President Grover Cleveland withdrew the annexation treaty, called the overthrow an 'act of war' and declined to restore the queen by force. The Republic of Hawaii was declared in 1894; Congress annexed it in 1898.",
 fact="Liliʻuokalani composed 'Aloha ʻOe', the best-known Hawaiian song, in 1878."),
"oc-nzsuffrage": dict(
 bluf="Suffrage campaigner Kate Sheppard and the Women's Christian Temperance Union set out to win New Zealand women the vote, over Premier Richard Seddon's opposition. Three petitions, the last signed by about a quarter of the colony's adult women, and a two-vote margin in the upper house carried the Electoral Act on 19 September 1893. New Zealand became the first self-governing country to give women the national vote, Māori women included, and other democracies followed over the next 30 years.",
 now="New Zealand's precedent gave suffrage campaigners elsewhere a working democracy to point to: Australia followed in 1902, Finland in 1906, Britain and the United States after the First World War.",
 story="The Women's Christian Temperance Union, with suffrage campaigner Kate Sheppard as its organiser, gathered petitions in 1891, 1892 and 1893; the last carried nearly 32,000 signatures, about a quarter of the colony's adult women. Premier Richard Seddon opposed the bill. The Legislative Council passed it by 20 votes to 18, and Governor Lord Glasgow signed the Electoral Act on 19 September 1893. Māori and Pākehā (European) women voted in the general election ten weeks later.",
 why="The temperance movement gave the campaign its organisation and a motive, since women were expected to vote against drink. Two opponents in the Council voted for the bill in anger at Seddon's tactics.",
 then="Australia granted women the federal vote in 1902 and Finland in 1906; Britain and the United States followed after the First World War. New Zealand women could stand for Parliament from 1919, and the first woman MP was elected in 1933.",
 fact="The 1893 petition was glued into a single roll about 270 metres long and unrolled on the floor of the House; Sheppard appears on New Zealand's $10 note."),
"na189856b": dict(
 bluf="US President William McKinley and the Senate's expansionists wanted Hawaii as a mid-Pacific naval base, sugar planters wanted its market secured, and the Republic of Hawaii set up by the 1893 coup wanted in. Congress annexed Hawaii by joint resolution in July 1898, during the war with Spain. The United States became a Pacific power with a base at Pearl Harbor, and Native Hawaiians lost their country without a vote.",
 now="Annexation made the United States a Pacific power and Pearl Harbor the base Japan struck in 1941; the US Congress apologised in 1993 for the overthrow that preceded it, and the apology is the basis of sovereignty claims.",
 story="The Republic of Hawaii, run by the planters who had overthrown Queen Liliʻuokalani in 1893, sought annexation from the day it was formed. A treaty failed to find two-thirds in the Senate. During the war with Spain, with the Philippines the next objective, Congress passed the Newlands Resolution in July 1898 and US President William McKinley signed it. The American flag went up over ʻIolani Palace on 12 August 1898.",
 why="The navy wanted a coaling and repair base at Pearl Harbor on the route to the Philippines. Sugar interests wanted the market secured for good.",
 then="Hawaii became a US territory in 1900. Pearl Harbor became the Pacific Fleet's main base, the target of Japan's attack in 1941, and Hawaii became a state in 1959.",
 fact="Annexation went through by simple majorities in both houses because a treaty could not get two-thirds of the Senate."),
"oc-federation": dict(
 bluf="Six colonies wanted common defence, a single market without internal tariffs and a national identity. After a decade of conventions and referendums they united on 1 January 1901 as the Commonwealth of Australia, a self-governing federation within the British Empire under Prime Minister Edmund Barton. The new democracy gave white men and, from 1902, white women the vote, and founded itself on racial exclusion: White Australia and the denial of the federal vote to most Aboriginal people.",
 now="The 1901 constitution still governs Australia and the British monarch is still head of state; a referendum on a republic failed in 1999 and one on an Indigenous Voice to Parliament failed in 2023.",
 story="The colonies of New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, South Australia, Western Australia and Tasmania negotiated a constitution at conventions in 1891 and 1897-98, and voters approved it in referendums between 1898 and 1900. The British Parliament enacted it in 1900. The Commonwealth was proclaimed in Centennial Park, Sydney, on 1 January 1901, with Governor-General Lord Hopetoun and Prime Minister Edmund Barton. Parliament sat in Melbourne until 1927.",
 why="Defence against Germany and France in the Pacific, trade without internal tariffs, and a growing sense of being Australian rather than six colonies.",
 then="The Immigration Restriction Act of 1901 created the White Australia policy. The Franchise Act of 1902 gave women the federal vote and excluded Aboriginal Australians, Asians, Africans and Pacific Islanders unless already enrolled in a state.",
 fact="Melbourne served as the federal capital until Parliament moved to Canberra in 1927."),
"oc-whiteaus": dict(
 bluf="The new federal parliament wanted to keep Australia white and its wages high, a goal shared across the main parties. The Immigration Restriction Act of 1901 let officials give any arrival a dictation test in a European language and so exclude Asian and other non-white migrants without naming race. The policy shaped Australia's population and its relations with Asia for 70 years, until governments dismantled it from 1966 and ended it in 1973.",
 now="White Australia kept the country British in population until the 1960s and strained its relations with Asia; its removal between 1966 and 1973 produced the Australia in which about 30 per cent of residents were born overseas.",
 story="The Immigration Restriction Act, one of the first laws of the Commonwealth Parliament sitting in Melbourne, passed in December 1901 under Prime Minister Edmund Barton. It let officials give any arrival a 50-word dictation test in a European language of the officer's choosing; the test was given to those the government wished to exclude, and nearly all of them failed it. A separate act provided for deporting Pacific Islander workers.",
 why="Fear of cheap Asian labour undercutting wages came first, and the labour movement and employers' parties agreed on it. A belief in a white nation ran through all of them, and London asked only that the law avoid naming race, to spare Japan's feelings.",
 then="Non-European migration stayed near zero for 60 years. The Holt government loosened the rules in 1966, and the Whitlam government removed race from immigration law in 1973.",
 fact="Czech journalist Egon Kisch, barred in 1934, was given the test in Scottish Gaelic, failed it, and won in the High Court, which ruled that Gaelic fell outside the act's meaning of a European language."),
"oc-gallipoli": dict(
 bluf="First Lord of the Admiralty Winston Churchill wanted to force the Dardanelles, take Constantinople and open a sea route to Russia. Allied troops, Australians and New Zealanders among them, landed on 25 April 1915, held a few square miles for eight months and withdrew; about 8,700 Australians and 2,700 New Zealanders died, with far larger numbers of British, French and Ottoman soldiers. The campaign failed, gave Australia and New Zealand a founding story of sacrifice, and made the reputation of Ottoman Lieutenant Colonel Mustafa Kemal.",
 now="Gallipoli gave Australia and New Zealand a national story independent of Britain and made the man who founded the Turkish Republic in 1923; both countries date their sense of nationhood from it.",
 story="Britain and France tried to force the Dardanelles by sea in March 1915 and lost three battleships to mines. British commander General Ian Hamilton then landed troops on the Gallipoli peninsula on 25 April 1915; the Australian and New Zealand Army Corps came ashore at what became Anzac Cove, British and French forces at Cape Helles. Ottoman Lieutenant Colonel Mustafa Kemal's 19th Division held the heights above the Anzac beach. After eight months of trench warfare the Allies withdrew in December 1915 and January 1916. About 8,700 Australians and 2,700 New Zealanders died, along with far larger numbers of British, French and Ottoman soldiers.",
 why="The Western Front was deadlocked and Churchill wanted a way round it. Taking Constantinople would have knocked the Ottomans out of the war and opened the Black Sea to Russia.",
 then="Churchill lost the Admiralty in May 1915. Kemal's reputation carried him to command of the Turkish national movement after 1919. The Allies shifted their effort to the Western Front, Salonika and Mesopotamia.",
 fact="The evacuation was the campaign's one success: more than 100,000 men were taken off the beaches with almost no losses."),
"na194167": dict(
 bluf="Japan, at war in China and cut off from American oil, set out to seize Southeast Asia's resources and to cripple the US Pacific Fleet so that it could not interfere. Japanese Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto's carriers struck Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941, sank or damaged eight battleships and killed about 2,400 Americans, and missed the carriers and the fuel stores. The attack brought the United States into the war with a united public, Germany declared war four days later, and the European and Asian wars became one.",
 now="The attack brought American industry into the war on both fronts, which decided it, and fixed the surprise attack as the scenario American defence planning has organised itself against ever since.",
 story="The United States froze Japanese assets and cut off oil in July 1941 after Japan occupied southern Indochina. Japanese Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto planned a carrier strike on the Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor; Vice Admiral Chūichi Nagumo's six carriers crossed the North Pacific undetected. On the morning of 7 December 1941 two waves of aircraft sank or damaged eight battleships and killed about 2,400 Americans. The three US carriers were at sea. Congress declared war the next day.",
 why="Japan needed oil for its war in China and judged the Dutch East Indies the source. The Pacific Fleet was the one force that could stop a southern advance, and Yamamoto believed a knockout blow would buy time to build a defensible perimeter.",
 trig="The US oil embargo and the failure of negotiations in November 1941, when Washington demanded that Japan withdraw from China.",
 then="Germany and Italy declared war on the United States on 11 December. American output, which exceeded that of all the Axis powers combined, tipped the war on both fronts. Japan's conquest of Southeast Asia ran to May 1942 and stopped at Midway in June.",
 fact="The sunken USS Arizona still leaks a few drops of oil every day, more than 80 years later."),
"oc-darwin": dict(
 bluf="Japan wanted to stop Darwin serving as a base against its invasion of the Dutch East Indies and Timor. On 19 February 1942, four days after Singapore fell, 242 aircraft from four of the carriers that had struck Pearl Harbor and from bases in the Dutch East Indies hit Darwin's harbour and airfields and killed at least 235 people. The attack confirmed that Britain could no longer defend Australia, and Australia built its security on the United States from then on.",
 now="Darwin fixed Australia's shift from Britain to the United States as guarantor, announced by Prime Minister John Curtin in December 1941; the US alliance, formalised in the ANZUS treaty of 1951, has been the core of Australian defence policy since.",
 story="Singapore surrendered on 15 February 1942. On 19 February, 188 aircraft from four Japanese carriers under Vice Admiral Chūichi Nagumo, led by Commander Mitsuo Fuchida, and 54 land-based bombers attacked Darwin in two raids. They sank eight ships, wrecked the airfields and killed at least 235 people; many residents and servicemen fled south in the days after. Raids on northern Australia continued until November 1943.",
 why="Japan wanted Darwin out of action before its landings on Timor and Java. The carriers were available between operations.",
 then="Curtin, who had declared in December 1941 that Australia 'looks to America', brought Australian divisions home from the Middle East and accepted US General Douglas MacArthur as supreme commander in the South-West Pacific in March 1942. Australia became the main US base in the South Pacific.",
 fact="Commander Mitsuo Fuchida led the first wave at Pearl Harbor and the raid on Darwin; the Australian government announced a far lower death toll at the time."),
"oc-guadalcanal": dict(
 bluf="The United States wanted to take the airfield Japan was building on Guadalcanal before it could cut the sea lanes to Australia; Japan wanted it back. US Marines under Major General Alexander Vandegrift landed on 7 August 1942 and held the field through six months of jungle fighting and night naval battles until Japan evacuated in February 1943. The attrition drained Japan's ships, aircraft and trained pilots, and the Allies took the initiative in the Pacific.",
 now="Guadalcanal showed that the Pacific War would be decided by shipyards, aircraft plants and pilot training, where the United States outproduced Japan many times over; from February 1943 the Allies chose where to fight, and Solomon Islanders who scouted for them were part of the reason.",
 story="Japanese forces began an airfield on Guadalcanal in the Solomon Islands in July 1942. US Marines under Major General Alexander Vandegrift landed on 7 August, took it and named it Henderson Field. Japan sent troops in by night, the 'Tokyo Express', and fought seven naval battles in the surrounding waters; so many ships sank that the strait became Ironbottom Sound. Solomon Islander scouts and Allied coastwatchers reported Japanese movements. Japan evacuated its surviving troops in the first week of February 1943.",
 why="The airfield would have put Japanese bombers over the convoy routes between the United States and Australia. The United States had the carriers left after Midway to cover a landing.",
 then="Japan lost two battleships, a carrier and hundreds of aircraft and pilots it could not replace. The Allies advanced up the Solomons and along New Guinea toward the Philippines.",
 fact="Solomon Islander Sergeant Major Jacob Vouza, captured, tied to a tree and bayoneted, freed himself and warned the Marines of an attack; he was knighted in 1979."),
"oc-kokoda": dict(
 bluf="Japan's South Seas Force under Major General Tomitarō Horii set out to take Port Moresby overland across the Owen Stanley Range after the sea route was blocked at the Coral Sea. Outnumbered Australian militia and then regular units fought a retreat along the Kokoda Track from July 1942, stopped the advance within 50 km of Port Moresby in September and drove the Japanese back to the north coast by November. Japan lost its last chance of bases within bombing range of Australia, and the campaign joined Gallipoli as a founding story.",
 now="Kokoda, with Milne Bay and Guadalcanal, ended Japan's advance in the South Pacific; it marks the point at which Australia's defence rested on its own troops and the United States rather than Britain.",
 story="Japanese forces landed at Gona on Papua's north coast on 21 July 1942 and pushed up the Kokoda Track across the Owen Stanley Range. Australian militia of the 39th Battalion, then regulars back from the Middle East, fought a retreat to Ioribaiwa Ridge, within 50 km of Port Moresby, in September. Horii's force, starving at the end of its supply line, was ordered back; the Australians retook Kokoda on 2 November and the fighting ended at Buna and Gona in January 1943. Papuan carriers, the 'Fuzzy Wuzzy Angels', carried supplies up the track and the wounded down it.",
 why="Port Moresby would have given Japan an airbase within range of northern Australia and the Coral Sea. The Coral Sea battle in May had turned back the seaborne attempt.",
 then="Australian and US forces took Buna and Gona in January 1943 at heavy cost. Japan's land advance in the South Pacific was over; Milne Bay in August-September 1942 had already been its first defeat in a landing.",
 fact="Horii drowned in the Kumusi River during the retreat in November 1942."),
"oc-midway": dict(
 bluf="Japanese Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto wanted to seize Midway and draw the remaining US carriers into a decisive battle; US Admiral Chester Nimitz, told by code-breakers where and when, set an ambush. On 4 June 1942 American dive bombers caught four Japanese carriers with aircraft refuelling on deck and sank all four, for the loss of one US carrier. Japan's offensive power in the Pacific was gone six months after Pearl Harbor, and the United States chose where to fight from then on.",
 now="Midway destroyed four Japanese carriers and ended Japan's offensive in the Pacific six months after Pearl Harbor; from then the United States chose where to fight.",
 story="Navy code-breakers at Pearl Harbor under Commander Joseph Rochefort read enough of Japan's naval code to identify Midway as the target and the date. US Admiral Chester Nimitz placed three carriers north-east of the atoll. On 4 June 1942, after torpedo squadrons had attacked at heavy loss, dive bombers from Enterprise and Yorktown found the carriers Akagi, Kaga and Sōryū with aircraft refuelling and rearming on deck and set all three ablaze within minutes; Hiryū was sunk that evening. The US lost the Yorktown.",
 why="Yamamoto wanted the US carriers destroyed before American shipyards could outbuild him. Nimitz knew the plan and accepted the odds.",
 then="Japan lost four fleet carriers and many of its best aircrew. The United States commissioned more than a dozen new fleet carriers by 1945 against Japan's handful, and went on the offensive at Guadalcanal in August 1942.",
 fact="The code-breakers confirmed the target by having Midway radio in clear that its water plant had failed; Japanese traffic soon reported that 'AF' was short of water."),
"oc-bikini": dict(
 bluf="The United States wanted to test its new atomic weapons, and their effect on warships, far from its own people on islands it had taken from Japan. It moved Bikini's 167 inhabitants in 1946, exploded two bombs over a target fleet, and carried out 67 nuclear tests in the Marshall Islands by 1958; the 15-megaton Castle Bravo shot of 1954 spread fallout over inhabited atolls and a Japanese fishing boat. Bikini remains uninhabited, and the tests made the Pacific the nuclear powers' proving ground and produced a regional anti-nuclear movement.",
 now="The tests displaced communities still unable to go home and made Pacific states the most consistent anti-nuclear bloc in the world: the Rarotonga Treaty of 1985 declared the South Pacific a nuclear-free zone, and Marshallese claims for compensation and clean-up remain open.",
 story="The US Navy asked Bikini's 167 inhabitants to leave in March 1946 and moved them to Rongerik atoll, where they went hungry. Operation Crossroads exploded two atomic bombs over a fleet of target ships in July 1946. The United States carried out 67 nuclear tests at Bikini and Enewetak by 1958. On 1 March 1954 the Castle Bravo hydrogen bomb, at 15 megatons the largest US test ever, spread fallout over Rongelap and Utirik and over the Japanese fishing boat Lucky Dragon No. 5, whose radio operator died.",
 why="The United States wanted to learn what atomic bombs did to warships and to develop thermonuclear weapons away from its own population. The Marshall Islands were under its control, as a UN trust territory from 1947.",
 then="Britain tested in Australia and at Christmas Island from 1952, and France at Moruroa and Fangataufa from 1966 to 1996. Bikinians returned in the 1970s and were evacuated again in 1978 when their bodies showed caesium. A 1986 compact gave the Marshall Islands independence and a compensation fund that the islands' claims tribunal later found far short of the damages it awarded.",
 fact="The bikini swimsuit, launched in Paris days after the first test, was named after the atoll."),
"oc-hawaii1959": dict(
 bluf="Hawaii's territorial leaders wanted statehood and the full representation that went with it; Southern Democrats in Congress had blocked it for years, in part because the islands' people were mostly Asian and Hawaiian. After Alaska's admission in January 1959 Congress passed the Admission Act, 94 per cent of voters said yes in June, and Hawaii became the 50th state on 21 August 1959. Statehood brought full citizenship and a jet-age tourism boom, and left unanswered the question Native Hawaiians were given no chance to vote on, independence.",
 now="Statehood settled Hawaii's place in the United States for most of its people and left the sovereignty question open: Native Hawaiian movements contest the annexation that preceded it, and the 1993 apology resolution acknowledged their claim without acting on it.",
 story="Hawaii had been a US territory since 1898, five years after American businessmen overthrew its queen, and had sought statehood since the 1930s. Southern Democrats in Congress blocked it, fearing two more senators for civil rights. Alaska was admitted in January 1959. US President Dwight Eisenhower signed the Hawaii Admission Act in March 1959; in a June referendum 94 per cent voted yes, and Hawaii became the 50th state on 21 August 1959. The ballot offered statehood or continued territorial status, with no option of independence.",
 why="Hawaii's place as the Pacific Fleet's base and its growing population of US citizens made continued territorial status hard to defend. Admitting Alaska first balanced the parties' fears about new senators.",
 then="Statehood brought full representation in Congress and a boom in jet-age tourism and migration from the mainland. The United Nations struck Hawaii from its list of non-self-governing territories in 1959.",
 fact="Daniel Inouye, a decorated Japanese-American veteran, became the state's first congressman in 1959 and sat in the Senate from 1963 until his death in 2012."),
"oc-samoa": dict(
 bluf="Samoans under the Mau movement wanted New Zealand's rule, imposed in 1914, to end; New Zealand, under UN pressure after 1945, wanted an orderly exit. Western Samoa became independent on 1 January 1962, the first Pacific island state to do so. It showed that small island nations could win sovereignty and opened a wave of decolonisation across the Pacific that ran to Fiji in 1970 and Papua New Guinea in 1975.",
 now="Samoa's independence set the pattern for a Pacific of small sovereign states, which is why the region today holds a dozen UN votes and is courted by Australia, China and the United States.",
 story="New Zealand took Western Samoa from Germany in August 1914 and ruled it under a League of Nations mandate and then a UN trusteeship. The nonviolent Mau movement campaigned for self-rule from the 1920s; on 28 December 1929, 'Black Saturday', New Zealand police fired on a Mau procession in Apia and killed its leader Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III and about ten others. After the Second World War New Zealand moved the territory toward self-government in stages. Western Samoa became independent on 1 January 1962 under Prime Minister Fiamē Mataʻafa Mulinuʻu II.",
 why="The Mau's long campaign kept the demand alive. The UN Trusteeship Council pressed New Zealand to set a date, and New Zealand wanted to leave on good terms.",
 then="Fiji became independent in 1970, Tonga ended its British protectorate the same year and Papua New Guinea followed in 1975. Western Samoa took the name Samoa in 1997. New Zealand Prime Minister Helen Clark apologised in 2002 for the 1918 influenza epidemic and Black Saturday.",
 fact="A New Zealand-administered ship carrying influenza was allowed to dock in 1918, and the epidemic killed about a fifth of Samoa's population; American Samoa, which imposed quarantine, lost no one."),
"oc-1967": dict(
 bluf="Aboriginal and non-Aboriginal campaigners wanted the Commonwealth empowered to make laws for Aboriginal people and to count them in the census, and Prime Minister Harold Holt's government agreed to put it to a vote. On 27 May 1967, 90.77 per cent of voters said yes, the largest majority in any Australian referendum. The federal government could now legislate on Indigenous affairs, which produced the land-rights laws of the 1970s and made Indigenous policy a national question.",
 now="The referendum made Indigenous affairs a Commonwealth responsibility, the base of every land-rights and native-title law since; in 2023 Australians rejected a proposal for an Indigenous Voice to Parliament in another referendum.",
 story="Australia's constitution excluded 'aboriginal natives' from the census count and barred the Commonwealth from making laws for them. A decade of campaigning by activists including Faith Bandler, Jessie Street and Charles Perkins, and the Freedom Ride through New South Wales towns in 1965, built support. Prime Minister Harold Holt's government put the amendment to a referendum on 27 May 1967. It passed with 90.77 per cent of the vote and carried every state.",
 why="A decade of campaigning by Aboriginal and non-Aboriginal activists came first. Australia was dismantling White Australia, and its treatment of Aboriginal people was an embarrassment abroad.",
 then="The Commonwealth legislated for Aboriginal people from the 1970s, including the Northern Territory land-rights act of 1976. The 2023 referendum on a Voice to Parliament failed with about 60 per cent voting no; its supporters held that a constitutional advisory body would give Indigenous Australians a say in the laws made about them, and its opponents held that it would divide citizens by race, that its design was left undefined and, for some Indigenous critics, that it offered too little.",
 myth="The referendum gave Aboriginal people citizenship and the vote. They were already citizens and could vote in federal elections from 1962; the vote changed the census and the Commonwealth's law-making power.",
 fact="Faith Bandler, the campaign's best-known organiser, was the daughter of a man taken from Vanuatu to Queensland's cane fields in the blackbirding trade."),
"oc-endwhite": dict(
 bluf="Prime Minister Gough Whitlam's government wanted race removed from immigration law to match Australia's trade with Asia and its standing abroad. In 1973 it ended the last racial criteria for entry, and the Racial Discrimination Act followed in 1975. Large-scale Asian migration began with Vietnamese refugees under the Fraser government in the late 1970s, and Australia became a country in which about 30 per cent of residents were born overseas.",
 now="The removal of race from immigration law produced the population Australia has now: about 30 per cent born overseas, one of the highest shares in the world, with India and China the largest sources after Britain.",
 story="The Holt government in 1966 had let non-Europeans with skills settle and shortened their wait for citizenship. Prime Minister Gough Whitlam's government, with Immigration Minister Al Grassby, ended the remaining racial criteria in 1973 and ratified international conventions against racial discrimination. The Racial Discrimination Act became law in 1975.",
 why="Trade with Japan and Asia had outgrown trade with Britain, which joined the European Community in 1973. Asian governments and Australia's own public had turned against the policy.",
 then="Prime Minister Malcolm Fraser's government took in Vietnamese refugees from 1976. Migration from Asia grew through the 1980s and 1990s.",
 fact="Grassby introduced the word 'multiculturalism' into Australian policy in a 1973 speech."),
"oc-png": dict(
 bluf="Chief Minister Michael Somare's Pangu Party wanted independence, and Australian Prime Minister Gough Whitlam wanted to hand over a territory Australia had run since 1906 before the UN or events forced him. Papua New Guinea became independent on 16 September 1975 with Somare as prime minister. Australian rule ended over the most linguistically diverse country on earth, with more than 800 languages, and the work of making one state of it, tested by the Bougainville war of 1988-98, continues.",
 now="Independence left a state of 800 languages with weak institutions, a secession war on Bougainville from 1988 to 1998, and a referendum in 2019 in which Bougainville voted 97.7 per cent for independence; negotiations with Port Moresby continue.",
 story="Australia governed Papua from 1906 and took German New Guinea in 1914, holding it under a League of Nations mandate and then a UN trusteeship. The two territories were administered together from 1949. Pangu Party leader Michael Somare became Chief Minister in 1972, the territory gained self-government in December 1973, and it became independent on 16 September 1975 with Somare as prime minister and the British monarch as head of state.",
 why="UN pressure for decolonisation and Whitlam's determination to leave came first. Somare's party had built a coalition across the regions.",
 then="A secession war on Bougainville, set off by grievances over the Panguna copper mine, ran from 1988 to 1998 and killed thousands. A peace agreement in 2001 led to the 2019 referendum.",
 fact="Papua New Guinea has more than 800 languages, more than a tenth of all those spoken in the world."),
"oc-rainbow": dict(
 bluf="France's DGSE intelligence service set out to stop Greenpeace sailing its ship Rainbow Warrior to protest against nuclear tests at Moruroa. Agents sank the ship with two mines in Auckland harbour on 10 July 1985 and killed photographer Fernando Pereira; two of them were caught and convicted in New Zealand. A state had bombed a civilian ship in a friendly port, and New Zealand answered by writing its nuclear-free policy into law in 1987 at the cost of its US alliance.",
 now="The bombing hardened New Zealand's nuclear-free stance into law in 1987 and cost it its place in the ANZUS alliance with the United States; an independent foreign policy has been part of national identity since.",
 story="The Rainbow Warrior was in Auckland preparing to lead a flotilla to Moruroa in French Polynesia. On the night of 10 July 1985 French DGSE agents attached two limpet mines to its hull; the second explosion killed Portuguese-born photographer Fernando Pereira. New Zealand police arrested two agents, Alain Mafart and Dominique Prieur, who pleaded guilty to manslaughter and received ten-year sentences. French Defence Minister Charles Hernu resigned and the head of the DGSE was dismissed after the French press exposed the operation.",
 why="France wanted the protests against its Pacific testing stopped and judged Greenpeace a threat to the programme.",
 then="Under a UN-mediated settlement in 1986 France apologised, paid New Zealand NZ$13 million and took the two agents to serve their sentences on Hao atoll; both were back in France by 1988. The United States had suspended its ANZUS obligations to New Zealand in 1986 over its ban on nuclear ships, and the Nuclear Free Zone Act of 1987 fixed the breach in law.",
 fact="The DGSE's name for the mission was Opération Satanique."),
"oc-fiji": dict(
 bluf="Fijian Lieutenant Colonel Sitiveni Rabuka set out to keep political power with Indigenous Fijians after an election had produced a government backed mainly by Indo-Fijians. His coup of 14 May 1987 removed Prime Minister Timoci Bavadra's government; a second coup in September made Fiji a republic outside the Commonwealth. Tens of thousands of Indo-Fijians emigrated, and the country entered a cycle of coups in 2000 and 2006 rooted in the colonial labour migration that had made it two peoples.",
 now="The coups fixed ethnicity as the organising fact of Fijian politics and shrank the Indo-Fijian population from nearly half to about a third; the coup leader of 1987 was elected prime minister in 1992 and again in 2022.",
 story="Britain brought about 60,000 indentured Indian labourers to Fiji's sugar plantations between 1879 and 1916, and by 1987 their descendants were nearly half the population. In April 1987 a coalition led by Fijian Prime Minister Timoci Bavadra, an Indigenous Fijian backed mainly by Indo-Fijian voters, won the election. On 14 May Lieutenant Colonel Sitiveni Rabuka led soldiers into Parliament and arrested the government. He staged a second coup on 25 September and declared a republic in October.",
 why="Indigenous Fijian nationalists feared losing land and political power to a population their own size. The army was almost entirely Indigenous Fijian.",
 then="A 1990 constitution reserved power for Indigenous Fijians, and tens of thousands of Indo-Fijians emigrated. Businessman George Speight held Parliament hostage in 2000, and Commodore Frank Bainimarama seized power in 2006 and ruled until 2022. Rabuka returned as elected prime minister in 1992 and in 2022.",
 fact="Rabuka's coup was the first military coup in the Pacific islands."),
"oc-mabo": dict(
 bluf="Meriam man Eddie Koiki Mabo and four others went to court in 1982 to prove that their people owned their land on Mer under their own law before and after the Crown arrived. On 3 June 1992 the High Court ruled six to one that 'terra nullius' was wrong as a description of Australia and that native title survived British sovereignty wherever it had not been extinguished. The ruling rewrote Australian land law, produced the Native Title Act of 1993 and gave the reconciliation debate its legal footing.",
 now="Native title has since been recognised over more than a third of Australia's land, most of it remote; the decision moved land and reconciliation politics onto a legal footing.",
 story="Eddie Koiki Mabo, a Meriam man working as a gardener at a Townsville university, learned in conversation with staff there that the law regarded his family's land on Mer as Crown land. With four other Meriam plaintiffs he brought a case in the High Court in 1982. Mabo died of cancer in January 1992. On 3 June 1992 the High Court ruled six to one that native title existed under Australian common law and that the Meriam people held it over Mer.",
 why="Indigenous campaigners challenged the legal basis of British possession, and the Meriam people's gardens and inherited plots gave the court a clear case of continuous ownership.",
 then="Parliament passed the Native Title Act in 1993 under Prime Minister Paul Keating. The Wik decision of 1996 held that native title could coexist with pastoral leases, and the Howard government's 1998 amendments narrowed it. Indigenous groups have since won recognition of native title over a large share of Australia's land, much of it remote.",
 fact="Mabo's grave in Townsville was vandalised in 1995, the night after its unveiling, and his family reburied him on Mer."),
"oc-apology": dict(
 bluf="Prime Minister Kevin Rudd set out to acknowledge in Parliament the removal of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children from their families between about 1910 and the 1970s, which his predecessor had declined to do. On 13 February 2008 the House passed the apology as the first business of the new Parliament. The apology settled the symbolic question and left the material ones open; gaps in health, schooling and imprisonment remain wide, and the scale and purpose of the removals are still argued.",
 now="The apology made the removals an acknowledged fact of national history and set 'Closing the Gap' targets for Indigenous health and education; the 2023 referendum on an Indigenous Voice to Parliament failed, so the question of a structural settlement remains open.",
 story="Between about 1910 and the 1970s state governments and missions removed Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children, many of mixed descent, from their families under protection and welfare laws. The 1997 'Bringing Them Home' inquiry report estimated that between one in ten and one in three Indigenous children were removed in that period and called for an apology. Prime Minister John Howard declined to give one. Prime Minister Kevin Rudd, elected in November 2007, apologised in the House of Representatives on 13 February 2008 with members of the Stolen Generations in the gallery.",
 why="Years of campaigning after the 1997 report, and a change of government in 2007, made the apology possible.",
 then="The government set 'Closing the Gap' targets in 2008. One reading holds the removals were a deliberate policy to absorb Aboriginal people into the white population and met the definition of genocide, as the 1997 report concluded; the other holds that most removals were welfare cases under laws applied to neglected children of every background and that the report's estimate overstates the number. Compensation schemes followed in several states, Tasmania first in 2006.",
 fact="The apology was the first item of business of the 42nd Parliament, and its text says 'we say sorry' three times."),
"oc-aukus": dict(
 bluf="Australia wanted submarines able to patrol far into the Pacific against a China whose navy had outgrown the region, and the United States and Britain wanted Australia bound into their effort to balance it. On 15 September 2021 Australian Prime Minister Scott Morrison, US President Joe Biden and British Prime Minister Boris Johnson announced AUKUS, under which Australia will acquire nuclear-powered submarines; Australia cancelled a French contract to do it. The pact tied Australian defence to the United States for decades and angered France and China.",
 now="AUKUS is the largest defence project in Australia's history, costed at A$268 to 368 billion over 30 years, and it commits Australia to the US position on China for the life of the submarines; after a Pentagon review in 2025 US President Donald Trump said it was going 'full steam ahead'.",
 story="Australia had contracted with France's Naval Group in 2016 for twelve conventional submarines. On 15 September 2021 Australian Prime Minister Scott Morrison, US President Joe Biden and British Prime Minister Boris Johnson announced AUKUS, a pact to give Australia nuclear-powered, conventionally armed submarines and to share technology in fields such as hypersonics and cyber. Australia cancelled the French contract the same day, and France recalled its ambassadors from Washington and Canberra. In March 2023 the three leaders set out the plan: US Virginia-class boats from the early 2030s and a new class built with Britain from the 2040s.",
 why="China's naval growth and its trade sanctions on Australia from 2020 convinced Canberra it needed longer-range submarines and a closer US tie. The United States wanted allied basing and shipbuilding in the Pacific.",
 then="China called the pact a threat to the regional arms balance and to the Non-Proliferation Treaty; the partners hold that the reactors will be sealed units and the boats will carry no nuclear weapons. A Pentagon review in 2025 endorsed the pact.",
 fact="France recalled its ambassador from Washington for the first time in the history of the alliance."),
"oc-falepili": dict(
 bluf="Tuvalu wanted a guaranteed future for its people as the sea rises over islands mostly less than 5 metres high; Australia wanted a security tie that would keep China out of a Pacific state. The Falepili Union, signed in November 2023 by Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese and Tuvaluan Prime Minister Kausea Natano, gives up to 280 Tuvaluans a year the right to live, work and study in Australia and gives Australia a say over Tuvalu's security arrangements with other countries. It is the first treaty to create a migration route because of climate change.",
 now="The treaty is the model for climate migration by agreement rather than flight, and it ties a Pacific microstate's security to Australia; more than 8,000 people applied in the first ballot in 2025 for 280 places, from a population of about 11,000.",
 story="Tuvalu's nine atolls hold about 11,000 people and rise mostly less than 5 metres above the sea. On 9 November 2023, at the Pacific Islands Forum in the Cook Islands, Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese and Tuvaluan Prime Minister Kausea Natano signed the Falepili Union. Australia offers up to 280 Tuvaluans a year permanent residence with the right to work and study, a guarantee to respond to natural disasters, health emergencies and military aggression, and a commitment to recognise Tuvalu's statehood as its land goes under. Tuvalu agreed to consult Australia on any security arrangement with another country. The treaty entered into force in August 2024.",
 why="Sea-level rise threatens Tuvalu's land and fresh water within decades. Australia wanted to pre-empt a Chinese security deal of the kind signed with Solomon Islands in 2022.",
 then="A new Tuvaluan government in 2024 reviewed the treaty and kept it. The first visa ballot in 2025 drew applications from more than 8,000 people.",
 fact="'Falepili' is the Tuvaluan word for the duties of good neighbours; Tuvalu's highest point is under 5 metres."),
}
